import{_t as e,it as t,n,rt as r}from"./chunks/framework.B5d4da8a.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Strategy C — Runtime Module Loading as Handler Interpreted Ops — design detail","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Strategy C — Runtime Module Loading as Handler Interpreted Ops — design detail","type":"design","date":"2026-10-16T00:00:00.000Z","updated":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","refs":["ADR-0009"]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"handl/project/design/0009-strategy-c-runtime-module-loading.md","filePath":"vendored/handl/project/design/0009-strategy-c-runtime-module-loading.md"}`),a={name:`handl/project/design/0009-strategy-c-runtime-module-loading.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return e(),r(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[t(`<h1 id="strategy-c-—-runtime-module-loading-as-handler-interpreted-ops-—-design-detail" tabindex="-1">Strategy C — Runtime Module Loading as Handler Interpreted Ops — design detail <a class="header-anchor" href="#strategy-c-—-runtime-module-loading-as-handler-interpreted-ops-—-design-detail" aria-label="Permalink to “Strategy C — Runtime Module Loading as Handler Interpreted Ops — design detail”">​</a></h1><p>Moved verbatim from ADR-0009 (ADR-0161 size budget). The ADR carries the rules; this page carries the context, tables, phases and trade-offs.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to “Context”">​</a></h2><p>ADR-0069 defines three normalisation strategies for module imports. Strategies A (compile-time desugar) and B (whole-program flattening) are implemented: they eliminate <code>module_ref</code>/<code>project</code> ops from the <code>Scheme</code> graph before the handler sees them. Strategy C (runtime module loading) is described but not implemented.</p><p>ADR-0069 §5 describes Strategy C as &quot;imports are resolved on first access&quot; via a runtime module loader. The original framing implied a separate mechanism — a runtime loader, a directive, a capability flag. That framing is wrong. It adds concepts the language doesn&#39;t need.</p><h3 id="the-insight" tabindex="-1">The insight <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-insight" aria-label="Permalink to “The insight”">​</a></h3><p>Module loading is already two <code>arr</code> ops:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>module_ref(&quot;collection/sort&quot;)  →  arr { op: &quot;module_ref&quot;, lit: &quot;collection/sort&quot; }</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>project(record, &quot;sort&quot;)        →  arr { op: &quot;project&quot;, args: [record], lit: &quot;sort&quot; }</span></span></code></pre></div><p>These are ordinary <code>arr</code> ops. A handler that has operation clauses for <code>module_ref</code> and <code>project</code> interprets them at runtime — loading the file, evaluating it, returning the arrow record, extracting the field. A handler that doesn&#39;t have those clauses either normalises them away first (Strategy A/B) or refuses to interpret the graph.</p><p><strong>The handler&#39;s operation table is the opt-in.</strong> No directive, no capability flag, no new mechanism. The same machinery that handles <code>arr add</code>, <code>arr const</code>, <code>arr list.head</code> handles <code>arr module_ref</code> and <code>arr project</code>.</p><h3 id="what-module-loading-actually-is" tabindex="-1">What module loading actually is <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-module-loading-actually-is" aria-label="Permalink to “What module loading actually is”">​</a></h3><p>Module loading is sugar for projection of evaluated code:</p><ul><li><code>module_ref(path)</code> = &quot;evaluate this file, give me the record of public arrows&quot;</li><li><code>project(record, field)</code> = &quot;give me this field from the record&quot;</li></ul><p>That&#39;s it. The handler&#39;s <code>module_ref</code> clause calls the resolver, evaluates the module, returns the record. The <code>project</code> clause extracts the field. Existing handler machinery, no new language concept.</p><h3 id="what-about-static-analysis" tabindex="-1">What about static analysis? <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-about-static-analysis" aria-label="Permalink to “What about static analysis?”">​</a></h3><p>A common objection to runtime loading: &quot;you lose static whole-program analysis.&quot; This is false. The <code>module_ref</code>/<code>project</code> ops are visible in the <code>Scheme</code> graph. You can:</p><ul><li><strong>Detect cycles statically:</strong> walk the graph, collect <code>module_ref(path)</code> nodes, build the import graph, detect cycles. No execution needed.</li><li><strong>Infer contracts:</strong> register <code>module_ref</code> and <code>project</code> in <code>ContractRegistry</code> with permissive contracts (input: Any, output: Any). For tighter contracts, load referenced modules&#39; contracts at analysis time and compose — without inlining code.</li><li><strong>Type check:</strong> the ops have types, the graph is closed, analysis works.</li></ul><p>What you lose is <em>precision</em> (permissive contracts for the ops vs. exact contracts for inlined arrows), not the capability itself. And precision is recoverable by loading referenced modules&#39; contracts without inlining their code.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to “Decision”">​</a></h2><p>Strategy C is <strong>not a separate strategy</strong>. It is what happens when you don&#39;t normalise <code>module_ref</code>/<code>project</code> away and the handler interprets them. The handler&#39;s operation table is the opt-in mechanism.</p><h3 id="_1-the-ops-are-the-mechanism" tabindex="-1">1. The ops are the mechanism <a class="header-anchor" href="#_1-the-ops-are-the-mechanism" aria-label="Permalink to “1. The ops are the mechanism”">​</a></h3><p><code>module_ref</code> and <code>project</code> are <code>arr</code> ops, already emitted by <code>rewrite_imports</code> in the parser. They are already in the <code>Scheme</code> graph. No new <code>Scheme</code> variants, no new language constructs.</p><p>A handler with operation clauses for these ops:</p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>handle module_ref(path) {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  -- resolve path, load file, evaluate, return record of public arrows</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  loader.load(path) &gt;&gt;&gt; evaluate</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span>
<span class="line"><span></span></span>
<span class="line"><span>handle project(record, field) {</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  -- extract field from record</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  record.field</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A handler without these clauses: the graph must be normalised (Strategy A/B) before interpretation, or the handler refuses with &quot;unknown op: module_ref&quot;.</p><h3 id="_2-no-directive-no-capability-flag" tabindex="-1">2. No directive, no capability flag <a class="header-anchor" href="#_2-no-directive-no-capability-flag" aria-label="Permalink to “2. No directive, no capability flag”">​</a></h3><p>The original Strategy C framing proposed:</p><ul><li>A <code>-- @strategy runtime</code> program directive → <strong>removed.</strong> The handler decides by having (or not having) clauses.</li><li>A <code>supports_runtime_loading</code> handler capability → <strong>removed.</strong> That&#39;s just &quot;does the operation table include <code>module_ref</code>?&quot;</li><li>A separate &quot;Strategy C&quot; code path → <strong>removed.</strong> It&#39;s the same graph, interpreted by a handler that handles the ops.</li></ul><h3 id="_3-governance-is-handler-configuration" tabindex="-1">3. Governance is handler configuration <a class="header-anchor" href="#_3-governance-is-handler-configuration" aria-label="Permalink to “3. Governance is handler configuration”">​</a></h3><p>The handler&#39;s <code>module_ref</code> clause is where governance lives:</p><ul><li><strong>Path allowlist:</strong> the clause checks <code>path</code> against <code>allowed_roots</code> before resolving. Paths outside → error.</li><li><strong>Depth limit:</strong> the clause tracks the active load stack. Exceeds <code>max_depth</code> → error.</li><li><strong>No network by default:</strong> which resolvers are wired into the handler&#39;s loader is handler config. <code>FileResolver</code> only by default; <code>NetworkResolver</code> only if explicitly configured.</li><li><strong>Cycle detection at runtime:</strong> the loader tracks the active load stack. A cycle → <code>LoadError::Cycle</code>, not an infinite loop.</li></ul><p>This is handler config — the same place every other capability is governed. No workspace-level policy file, no program-level directive.</p><h3 id="_4-static-analysis-passes-no-execution-needed" tabindex="-1">4. Static analysis passes (no execution needed) <a class="header-anchor" href="#_4-static-analysis-passes-no-execution-needed" aria-label="Permalink to “4. Static analysis passes (no execution needed)”">​</a></h3><p>Two static passes over the <code>Scheme</code> graph, both already implementable with the current op encoding:</p><p><strong>Cycle detection:</strong></p><div class="language-"><button title="Copy code" data-copied="Copied" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes handl-dark github-light" style="--shiki-dark:#ffffff;--shiki-light:#24292e;--shiki-dark-bg:#2a2a2a;--shiki-light-bg:#fff;" tabindex="0" dir="ltr"><code><span class="line"><span>fn detect_cycles(scheme: &amp;Scheme) -&gt; Result&lt;(), CycleError&gt;</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  - walk scheme, collect all module_ref(path) nodes</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  - build import graph: path → set of paths it imports</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>  - detect cycles via DFS</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong>Contract inference:</strong></p><ul><li>Register <code>module_ref</code> and <code>project</code> in <code>ContractRegistry</code> with permissive contracts.</li><li>For tighter contracts: load referenced modules&#39; contracts (via <code>ModuleLoader</code>&#39;s contract cache), compose without inlining code.</li><li><code>infer_refinement</code> works on the whole graph without modification.</li></ul><h3 id="_5-the-scheme-variant-question" tabindex="-1">5. The <code>Scheme</code> variant question <a class="header-anchor" href="#_5-the-scheme-variant-question" aria-label="Permalink to “5. The Scheme variant question”">​</a></h3><p><code>module_ref</code>/<code>project</code> now reach interpretation under this design. They remain <code>arr</code> ops, not real <code>Scheme</code> variants. The handler matches on <code>op: &quot;module_ref&quot;</code> stringly, same as every other op.</p><p>Graduate to real <code>Scheme::ModuleRef</code>/<code>Scheme::Project</code> variants only if:</p><ul><li>A second handler needs to interpret them (one handler matching a string is fine; two duplicating the convention is the signal), AND</li><li>The stringly-typed matching causes real problems (bugs, missed cases).</li></ul><p>Until then, the closed kernel principle (ADR: closed arrow calculus kernel) holds: six combinators, no more.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to “Consequences”">​</a></h2><p><strong>Positive:</strong></p><ul><li>No new language mechanism. Module loading uses the existing handler <code>op</code> machinery. Zero new concepts.</li><li>Opt-in is automatic: a handler that handles <code>module_ref</code>/<code>project</code> does runtime loading; one that doesn&#39;t doesn&#39;t. No configuration beyond the operation table.</li><li>Static analysis is preserved. Cycle detection and contract inference work on the graph without execution.</li><li>Enables REPLs, hot-reload, plugin systems, dynamic loading — all via handler clauses, no special-casing.</li><li>Composes with Strategies A/B: normalise when you want a closed artifact, don&#39;t normalise when you want runtime loading. Same graph, different handler.</li></ul><p><strong>Negative:</strong></p><ul><li>Less precise contracts than inlining (permissive <code>Any</code> contracts for the ops vs. exact contracts for inlined arrows). Recoverable by loading referenced modules&#39; contracts without inlining code.</li><li>Runtime loading overhead (amortised by the module loader&#39;s cache).</li><li>The handler must implement governance (allowlist, depth, cycle detection) in its <code>module_ref</code> clause. This is handler code, not language machinery — but it&#39;s still code someone must write and get right.</li></ul><p><strong>Risk:</strong></p><ul><li>Handler authors must implement governance correctly. A handler that resolves <code>module_ref</code> without checking the allowlist or depth limit is a security hole. Mitigation: provide a standard <code>module_ref</code> clause implementation in the stdlib that handles governance, so handler authors don&#39;t write it from scratch.</li></ul><h2 id="implementation-what-s-left" tabindex="-1">Implementation (what&#39;s left) <a class="header-anchor" href="#implementation-what-s-left" aria-label="Permalink to “Implementation (what&#39;s left)”">​</a></h2><p>The infrastructure already exists from ADR-0069 implementation:</p><ul><li><code>ModuleResolver</code> trait + <code>FileResolver</code>/<code>MemoryResolver</code>/<code>ChainResolver</code>/<code>CachingResolver</code> — done</li><li><code>ModuleLoader</code> with graph + contract caches — done</li><li><code>module_ref</code>/<code>project</code> op encoding (<code>rewrite_imports</code>) — done</li><li><code>normalize_compile_time</code> / <code>flatten_program</code> (Strategies A/B) — done</li></ul><p>What&#39;s left:</p><ol><li><strong>Handler <code>module_ref</code>/<code>project</code> clauses</strong> — a standard implementation in the stdlib that calls the resolver, evaluates, returns the record/field, with governance (allowlist, depth, cycle detection).</li><li><strong><code>module_ref</code>/<code>project</code> contracts in <code>ContractRegistry</code></strong> — permissive contracts for static inference without inlining.</li><li><strong>Static cycle detection pass</strong> — walk the graph, collect <code>module_ref</code> nodes, build import graph, detect cycles.</li><li><strong>Handler config for governance</strong> — allowlist, depth limit, resolver wiring.</li></ol><h2 id="relationship-to-adr-0069" tabindex="-1">Relationship to ADR-0069 <a class="header-anchor" href="#relationship-to-adr-0069" aria-label="Permalink to “Relationship to ADR-0069”">​</a></h2><p>This ADR refines ADR-0069 §5 Strategy C. It does not contradict ADR-0069 — the semantic model (import = projection) is unchanged. It clarifies the implementation: Strategy C is not a separate mechanism but the handler interpreting ops that Strategies A/B would eliminate. ADR-0069&#39;s description of Strategy C as &quot;imports are resolved on first access&quot; is accurate; this ADR specifies <em>how</em> (handler clauses, not a directive or capability flag).</p>`,57)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};